	stays; -markstyle font still overrides.
	* src/cat.tcl (FormUnquoteString, getForms): a text value comes back
	unpacked, the way fillForms takes it in; it used to come back raw, so
	reading a form and writing it back doubled the escaping of every
	untouched field on every pass. A name value (/Yes, /Off) is left
	alone. Literal strings, hex strings, octal escapes and UTF-16BE with
	a byte order mark are handled (ISO 32000 clauses 7.3.4.2, 7.3.4.3 and
	7.9.2.2).
	* src/cat.tcl (FormSetKey): the pattern for a string value stopped at
	the first escaped bracket, so replacing a value that contained one
	left the rest of the old string behind -- the other half of the round
	trip defect above. It now matches escaped pairs.
	* src/main.tcl: the catalog /Version entry is written as a NAME.
	ISO 32000 clause 7.7.2 gives the type as name; pdf4tcl wrote a number,
	in every file above version 1.4 -- so in all PDF/A-2 and -3 output
	since those levels exist. The files stayed readable; a reader taking
	the version from the catalog rather than the header got the wrong
	type. Found by tools/pdfcheck-native.tcl over examples/, which
	reported it on two of fifteen files.
	* tools/pdfcheck-native.tcl (checkInfoXmp): reads /Info through the
	trailer rather than searching the whole file for /Title. That key is
	not unique in a PDF -- every bookmark carries one -- so in a document
	with an outline the first hit was the first bookmark. Measured on
	tutorial-06-pack-body.pdf: five /Title entries, the document's own
	last, and the check reported Info "Cover" against XMP "Reading pack"
	when both were right. A real mismatch is still reported.
	* src/main.tcl (_BuildXMPStream): pdf:Producer names the release. The
	property is an AgentName (XMP part 2 table 30) and the recommended
	form for one is "Organization Software Version" (part 1 clause
	8.2.2.1); it used to be the bare word "pdf4tcl", which says nothing
	about which release wrote a file -- the first thing worth knowing when
	a document from elsewhere turns out to be wrong. -producer still
	overrides it.
	* src/main.tcl (_BuildXMPStream): xmp:MetadataDate is written beside
	xmp:ModifyDate. The one records when the metadata last changed, the
	other when the content did (part 1 table 5); the first shall be the
	same as or later than the second, and the same value is the honest
	answer at write time. Absent when no modification date was given --
	inventing a date nobody set would be worse than none.
	* tests/pdfa.test: mark-1.4 pinned the old behaviour on the assumption
	that "the glyph is not what makes it fail"; that assumption was wrong
