2026-08-23 Gregor  <gregnix@github>

	* Bumped revision to 0.9.4.54
	* src/main.tcl: two objects were written straight out with Pdfout and
	so bypassed EncryptStringsInBody -- the name tree of the embedded
	files and the OCG dictionaries of the layers. ISO 32000-2 clause 7.6.2
	lists four exceptions from encryption (trailer /ID, strings in the
	encryption dictionary, strings inside already encrypted streams, and a
	signature's Contents); neither of these is among them.
	In an encrypted file /F and /UF of the file specification were
	encrypted while the name in the name tree stayed in the clear, so the
	two no longer matched: "qpdf --list-attachments" printed an empty name
	and "--show-attachment=anhang.txt" answered "attachment not found".
	The attachment was unreachable. Without encryption both worked. This
	cannot arise under PDF/A, which forbids encryption altogether; it
	affects attachments in an encrypted document that claims no
	conformance.
	Pointed out indirectly by Alexander Schoepe (tclpdf), whose remark
	about the EFF entry led here.
	* src/main.tcl (CheckPdfaEncrypt): -pdfa together with a password is
	refused. Every PDF/A profile carries the rule at clause 6.1.3 test 2:
	"The keyword Encrypt shall not be used in the trailer dictionary" in
	parts 1 to 3, "The Encrypt key shall not be present in the trailer
	dictionary" in part 4 -- no level, no exception. Read from the veraPDF
	1.28 validation profiles. pdf4tcl let the combination through, and the
	resulting file carried its pdfaid claim beside an /Encrypt entry.
	veraPDF 1.30.2 on such a file answers "appears to be an encrypted PDF
	file and could not be processed" -- it never gets far enough to refute
	the claim.
	* demo/demo-aes256.tcl and the three other encryption demos that
	suggest "qpdf --check": they now say that qpdf 12 warns about /Length
	and exits 3, and why that is expected. The demos invited the check;
	leaving the reader to wonder about the warning would be worse than
	the warning itself.
	* tests/encrypt.test: 5 tests for the encrypted strings, tests/pdfa.test
	2 for the refusal. encrypt-empty-1.2 and 1.3 measured the encrypted
	empty appearance stream and needed -pdfa and a password at once; that
	is now refused, and without -pdfa addForm writes no appearance XObject
	at all, so the subject no longer exists. Replaced by one test that
	pins the refusal.

2026-08-22 Gregor  <gregnix@github>

	* Bumped revision to 0.9.4.53
	* src/encrypt.tcl: no /Length in the encryption dictionary for V 4
	and V 5. Both standards list the entry as "Optional; PDF 1.4; only if
	V is 2 or 3" -- ISO 32000-1 Table 20 as well as ISO 32000-2, which
	adds "deprecated in PDF 2.0". pdf4tcl wrote /Length 256 beside /V 5
	and /Length 128 beside /V 4, where neither belongs; for V 5 the key
	length is part of the algorithm, for V 4 it sits in the crypt filter.
	The /Length 32 and /Length 16 inside /CF are a different table
	(crypt filter dictionary) and stay.
	Pointed out by Alexander Schoepe (tclpdf) on 2026-08-22, who had the
	same entry and removed it.
	KNOWN: qpdf reads the entry unconditionally, so a conforming file now
	draws a warning from it. Measured on both versions: qpdf 11.9.0 gives
	rc=0 and stays quiet, qpdf 12.4.0 gives rc=3 with
	"dictionary key /Length: operation for integer attempted on object of
	type null: returning 0" and "operation succeeded with warnings".
	It is a warning, not an error -- the check completes and the file
	decrypts either way, in qpdf as well as in pdftotext. Anyone testing
	the exit status of "qpdf --check" on an encrypted pdf4tcl file will
	see it fail from 0.9.4.53 on. The standard is followed rather than the
	tool.
	* tools/bump.tcl: README.md gets only its title line rewritten, not
	every occurrence of the version. The other mentions there are
	historical -- "Since 0.9.4.36", "fixed in 0.9.4.49" -- and one of them
	had been travelling: the line about the ticket*/ directories said
	0.9.4.45 correctly and then followed eight bumps up to 0.9.4.53. The
	next one in line would have been "fixed in 0.9.4.53" in the section on
	where the parts come from.
	* tests/encrypt.test (encrypt-2.3), tests/new-0.9.4.16.test
	(encrypt-9.4): both pinned the entry that should not be there; they
	now pin its absence. The AES-256 tests are skipped by default for
	time reasons -- run once with testConstraint aes256 1: 21 of 21.

