2026-08-20 Gregor  <gregnix@github>

	* Bumped revision to 0.9.4.50
	* src/cat.tcl (DropUnreachable): objects nothing points at are removed
	after merging. Every input's /Info was taken over while the trailer
	names one, so the others stayed behind -- no reader saw them, but a
	grep found a title belonging to nothing. Reachability from the
	trailer rather than a list of types, so anything else the merge leaves
	behind is caught too. Pages and structure elements are kept whatever
	the scan says.
	* src/cat.tcl (WritePdf): /Size is derived from N rather than taken
	from the trailer. AppendPdf set both, but every later step changed the
	object count and carried only N, leaving a /Size that qpdf reported as
	not one plus the highest object number. Present before this release;
	the entry above made it visible.
	* tests/cat-acroform.test: 33 tests.
	* src/cat.tcl (getForms): a widget without /T no longer aborts the
	read. With radio buttons the parent carries the name and the children
	only their appearance; getForms failed with "key /T not known in
	dictionary". Found while extending demo/demo-forms.tcl, not by the
	suite -- no test had read a radio group back.
	* tests/form-ext.test: 77 tests.
	* demo/demo-forms.tcl: finds the package by searching for pkgIndex.tcl
	next to src/ rather than counting three directories upwards, which
	pointed outside the tree and loaded whatever pdf4tcl happened to sit
	there. Also shows getForms, fillForms and what fillForms does not do.
	* doc/en/howtos/howto-forms.md, howto-facturx.md, howto-catpdf.md:
	reading and filling forms, Order-X beside Factur-X, and object streams
	-- including the trap that qpdf without --newline-before-endstream
	writes input that fails PDF/A before pdf4tcl sees it.
	* src/main.tcl (getStringWidth): the kerning was added twice.
	ShapedWidth already includes it, and the older per-character block
	below was left in place outside the if/else, so it applied to the
	shaped path as well. A string measured narrower than it was drawn --
	the opposite of what 0.9.4.49 set out to fix. Present in 0.9.4.49 as
	released; kerning-4.3 and kerning-6.4 report it.
	* src/main.tcl (orderx): attaches an electronic order as Order-X, the
	ordering counterpart of Factur-X. Namespace, file name and document
	types differ; the profiles are basic, comfort and extended rather than
	the invoice levels, and each set is refused by the other method.
	Everything else is delegated to facturx, which now takes the list of
	valid profiles as an argument, so attachment, XMP block and /AF
	relationship stay in one place.
	* src/cat.tcl (UnpackObjStreams, UndoPngPredictor): catPdf reads
	objects out of object streams (/ObjStm, ISO 32000-1 clause 7.5.7).
	Type 2 entries in the cross-reference table used to be refused with a
	count; the container is now decompressed and its header of number and
	offset pairs read. Files with a cross-reference stream use object
	streams by default, so this was the second half of what 0.9.4.49
	started.
	The rows of a cross-reference stream may carry a PNG row filter
	(/DecodeParms with /Predictor 12, which is what qpdf writes by
	default). Without undoing it every row after the first is offset, and
	the file still parses -- the error shows up as an object number that
	cannot exist rather than as a failure.
	* tests/cat-acroform.test: 27 tests.
	* src/main.tcl (startXObject): a form XObject now names its resource
	dictionary in every case, not only with -noimage. ISO 19005-2 clause
	6.2.2 wants a content stream that references fonts or images to carry
	an explicitly associated Resources dictionary; without it a PDF/A-3a
	file with a tagged XObject passed PDF/UA-1 and failed 3a at that
	clause alone.
	* tests/pdfa.test: 71 tests.
	* src/cat.tcl (fillForms): fills the form fields of an existing PDF and
	writes it out again -- the counterpart to getForms, which could read
	them but not write. A field named but not present raises an error; a
	form that comes out empty with no explanation is worse than a refused
	call. Check boxes take the state name and get /AS as well, or the box
	keeps its old appearance. /NeedAppearances is set: the value is
	written, not drawn.
	* tests/form-ext.test: 8 tests, 75 in total.
	* KNOWN: fillForms writes no appearance stream. A viewer that ignores
	/NeedAppearances shows the field as it was, with the value present but
	invisible.
	* doc/en/howtos/howto-tagged.md, howto-xobject.md: the two topics that
	were built and tested but had no recipe. Both with a runnable script;
	the tagged one passes veraPDF as PDF/UA-1.
	* tests/facturx.test: 6 tests for Order-X, 25 in total.
	* tools/mk-feature-matrix.tcl: prints which of the documented topics
	have a reference entry, a howto with a runnable script, a tutorial, a
	demo, an example and a test. Names are searched rather than listed, so
	a topic added later appears without the tool being edited; a test that
	carries a different name is found through a synonym list. Reports
	tagged PDF and XObject tagging as having no howto.

2026-08-19 Gregor  <gregnix@github>

	* Bumped revision to 0.9.4.49
	* src/helpers.tcl (CIDEncodeText): a codepoint the font has no glyph
	for is now counted. It was drawn as GlyphID 0 and passed over, so
	getSubstCount -- which the subset path has fed since 0.9.4.35 --
	stayed at zero for CID fonts, which are the ones used for text outside
	Latin-1.
	* src/helpers.tcl (NoteMissingGlyph): both paths now append to
	::pdf4tcl::warnings, naming the codepoint and what was drawn in its
	place -- a question mark on the subset path, .notdef on the CID path.
	Once per font and codepoint, so a page of Chinese in a Latin face
	gives a handful of lines rather than one per character; the counter
	still counts every occurrence. A warning rather than an error, so a
	document that puts up with the substitution keeps working.
	The defect was pointed out in a comparison with tclpdf posted to a
	newsgroup on 2026-08-18, where it was named correctly: until then a
	character the font could not draw disappeared without a word. No code
	was taken over; what came from there is the finding.
	* tests/new-0.9.4.35.test: 5 tests added, 42 in total.
	* src/cat.tcl (ReadXrefStream): catPdf reads cross-reference streams
	(ISO 32000-1 clause 7.5.8). Such a file has no "trailer" keyword --
	the dictionary sits in the stream object and the table is packed into
	its data, decoded per /W and /Index. PDF/A-1 forbids the form and
	PDF/A-2 and -3 require it, so until now no archival document from 2b
	upwards and no ZUGFeRD invoice could be merged at all. Measured: two
	PDF/A-2b files merge and the result passes veraPDF as 2b; two tagged
	PDF/A-3a files merge and the result passes 3a and UA-1.
	Only /FlateDecode is decoded; another filter is named rather than
	turned into nonsense. Objects inside an object stream (/ObjStm, type 2
	in the table) are not unpacked, and a file carrying them is refused
	with the count rather than losing them silently.
	* tests/cat-acroform.test: 25 tests, and the files they write are
	removed again -- thirteen were left in the working directory, which is
	noise the next run has to look past.
	* src/main.tcl (WriteXrefStream): the stream now describes itself. Its
	object number was taken after /Size was computed, so a file with
	objects 1..15 said "/Size 15" and the table covered 0..14 -- the stream
	object was missing from its own table (ISO 32000-1 clause 7.5.8.2).
	Neither qpdf --check nor veraPDF reported it.
	* tests/pdfa.test: 4 tests for that, 70 in total. One reads the entry
	out of the stream and checks that it points at the object header.
	* src/helpers.tcl (ShapeRun, ShapedWidth, EncodeGlyphSlice): measuring
	and drawing now share one shaped glyph run. PdfTextKerned split the
	string on the glyphs BEFORE ligature substitution and passed the
	ligature flag only to the last piece, so "ffi" in Carlito came out as
	f + kern + fi and not the ffi glyph -- the kerning split undid
	"longest match wins" from ApplyLigatures. Order is now Unicode ->
	glyphs -> ligatures -> kerning -> TJ.
	* src/main.tcl (getStringWidth): measures that same run. Ligatures were
	not considered at all, so a line was measured wider than it was drawn:
	in Carlito f+f+i is 839.8 against 807.6 for the ffi glyph, four
	percent, which moves centred text and breaks lines in the wrong place.
	The advance of a ligature comes from the horizontal metrics, indexed by
	glyph id -- the same source the /W array is built from. A codepoint the
	font cannot represent counts as the width of '?', which is what gets
	drawn. Fonts without metrics fall back to the per-character sum.
	* src/helpers.tcl (ShapeRun, EncodeGlyphSlice): a missing glyph is
	reported on the kerned path too. ShapeRun had decoupled it from
	CIDEncodeText, so whether a lost character was counted depended on
	whether the string happened to kern anywhere. The codepoint is now kept
	alongside GlyphID 0 and the encoder counts it, not the shaper --
	ShapeRun also serves getStringWidth, and measuring must not raise the
	counter.
	* tests/new-0.9.4.35.test: 3 tests for that, 45 in total.
	* src/main.tcl (SetPageMargin): the unreachable default branch throws
	instead of printing a debug line.
	* src/fonts.tcl, src/helpers.tcl: the IgnoreMarks lookup flag is kept
	per entry rather than per font. One GPOS lookup with bit 3 used to set
	a font-wide flag, after which every pair skipped marks -- including
	pairs from lookups without the flag and pairs from the kern table,
	which has no lookup flags at all. GetKernPairInfo returns the value
	and the flag of the lookup the pair came from; NextKernGlyph tries the
	immediate neighbour first and steps over a mark only when the pair
	beyond it asks for it. IsMarkGlyph reports the GDEF class alone;
	IsSkippableMark stays for outside callers.
	* tests/kerning.test: 31 tests.
	* KNOWN: catPdf -title writes /Info only. The 0.9.4.48 entry called
	this unreachable while xref streams were unsupported; they are read
	now, so a merged PDF/A-2b carries the XMP title of its first part
	while /Info says something else, and PDF/A wants the two to agree.
	* KNOWN: a glyph carries one ToUnicode mapping, but a face may use the
	same glyph for U+FB01 and for the ligature formed from f and i. The
	last use wins, so a document containing both extracts one of the two
	wrongly.
	* tests/ligatures.test: 16 tests. ligatures-3.4 checked only that some
	TJ or Tj had been written -- it stayed green with no ligature formed
	anywhere, which is why the two entries above went unnoticed. It now
	checks for the ffi glyph, with "ffi" rather than "Auflage" because the
	kerning between the two f is what broke it.

2026-08-19 Gregor  <gregnix@github>

	* Bumped revision to 0.9.4.48
	* src/cat.tcl: catPdf takes -title, -author, -subject, -keywords,
	-creator and -producer, before the file names. Merging keeps the
	catalog of the first input and with it its /Info, so a merged file
	otherwise carries the title of part one. An empty value removes the
	entry, entries not named keep what the first document had, and
	brackets and backslashes are escaped -- an unescaped bracket ends the
	string and the object with it. SetInfo runs after DedupObjects, or a
	rewritten /Info would be folded back onto the original. The file names
	stay positional, so existing calls are unaffected.
	* src/cat.tcl (ReadPdf): a cross-reference stream is reported instead
	of failing inside the parser with an unset trailertxt. PDF/A-1 forbids
	xref streams (ISO 19005-1) and PDF/A-2 and -3 require them, so no
	document from 2b upwards can be merged; 1b can. A missing startxref is
	reported as well.
	* tests/cat-acroform.test: 12 tests added, 23 in total.
	* pdf4tcl.man: the new options.
	* KNOWN: the /Info of an appended document stays in the file
	unreferenced.
	* KNOWN: /Metadata (XMP) is not written, so a reader preferring XMP
	shows the title of the first document. PDF/A requires the two to
	agree; unreachable while xref streams are unsupported.

